          "text": "**Observations**\n\n- The largest first‑layer weights are not in the middle of the image but at its very edges. Image row 0 (inputs 0–27) carries weights up to ±0.30 (e.g. unit 1: +0.28 at cols 9–11 and +0.27 at col 17, −0.29/−0.30 at cols 13–15; unit 10: −0.13/−0.21 at cols 10–11), row 1 has the single largest weight in the network (unit 10: −0.47 at col 17, −0.36 at col 10), and rows 2–3 continue the pattern at cols 8–9 and 18–23. So the top edge, centre columns, is highly informative – the typical picture is of something that touches the top of the frame with a dip in the middle (a neck‑line between two shoulders, or a waistband).\n- The bottom edge is likewise informative: row 27 has unit 10 = +0.32/+0.42 at cols 13–14, unit 5 = +0.25 at col 8, unit 1 = +0.11 at cols 20–21; rows 24–26 repeat this (unit 10 ≈ +0.15…+0.35 at cols 13–14, unit 0 = −0.18 at col 20). \n- In the lower half (rows ~15–27) there are clean vertical strips: at column ~5 units 12 and 15 are strongly positive (+0.18…+0.28 and +0.21…+0.23), at column 14 units 5, 6, 9 are negative (≈ −0.15…−0.21) while unit 10 is positive (+0.10…+0.42), and at columns 20–21 unit 0 is positive while unit 12 is negative (−0.20). That is a \"left leg / gap / right leg\" template.\n- Corners and extreme left/right columns are near initialisation noise (|w| < 0.04), as are a few mid‑image areas; the units are coarse, blobby silhouette templates rather than stroke detectors. Several units have large positive biases (unit 5: 0.79, unit 14: 0.73) and mostly negative input weights, i.e. \"default on, switched off when the image is heavily filled\" – sparseness detectors.\n- Output layer, reading hidden units as silhouette features: unit 1 (top‑edge shoulders‑with‑dip) → classes 0, 2, 1, 3 positive, classes 4, 5, 9, 8 negative. Unit 0 (top centre empty, right‑lower side filled) → classes 6, 7, 8, 9 positive, 0, 1, 3 negative. Unit 10 (bottom‑centre column filled, top shoulders empty) → 6, 3, 0 positive, 1, 2 negative. Unit 15 (lower‑left column filled) → 1, 4, 5, 2 positive, 9, 7, 0 negative. Unit 5 (sparse / central gap) → 5, 1 positive, 9, 4, 6 negative. Unit 14 (sparse) → 8 strongly negative. Unit 2 (top centre filled) → 4, 3, 2 positive, 8, 5, 7 negative.\n\n**Reasoning**\n\nAny MNIST‑style dataset (MNIST digits, EMNIST letters, KMNIST) centres a ~20×20 glyph in the 28×28 frame, so rows 0–3 and 24–27 are essentially always zero; weights there never receive gradient and stay at their tiny initial values. Here the opposite holds: the biggest weights in the whole network sit on rows 0–1 and 26–27 at the centre columns. The objects therefore routinely touch the top and bottom borders, which is exactly the Fashion‑MNIST preprocessing (object scaled so its longest side fills 28 px). The learned features are silhouette parts – shoulders/neckline at the top, hem at the bottom, two legs with a gap in the lower half, \"mostly empty\" detectors – not strokes, which again argues for clothing items rather than handwriting. Natural‑image sets (CIFAR, SVHN) are excluded by the 28×28 single‑channel format.\n\nThe output weights match the standard Fashion‑MNIST label order remarkably well: the neckline unit favours T‑shirt/Pullover/Dress and is negative on Sandal/Boot/Bag (items that don't reach the top centre); the \"top empty\" unit favours Sneaker, Bag, Ankle boot; the \"bottom‑centre column filled / no leg gap\" unit is positive for Dress/T‑shirt/Shirt and negative for Trouser; the lower‑left‑column unit (long leg or sleeve) is positive for Trouser/Coat/Pullover and negative for footwear and T‑shirt; the sparseness unit favours Sandal and Trouser and is negative for Ankle boot and Coat; another sparseness unit is strongly negative for Bag (the most solidly filled class).\n\n**Dataset:** Fashion‑MNIST (Zalando 28×28 grayscale clothing articles, 10 classes, standard label order).\n\n**Classes:** 0 T‑shirt/top, 1 Trouser, 2 Pullover, 3 Dress, 4 Coat, 5 Sandal, 6 Shirt, 7 Sneaker, 8 Bag, 9 Ankle boot.\n\n**Confidence:** 85%",
